Raise package attempts 20%, from 800 to 960, while yield stays 90%. That creates 864 working packages, but only 660 can be deployed. The extra packages become inventory rather than useful AI.

These investments could matter later, but they do not meet the near-term target.

5. Build a coordinated answer

Suppose deployment rises from 660 to the existing package output of 720 accelerators. At the same time, better scheduling, data delivery and workload software raise useful utilization from 70% to 77%. Availability and per-useful-hour performance stay unchanged.

720 × 95% × 77% × 100 = 52,668 work units

That is exactly 20% above baseline. Deployment improves 9.1%, and useful utilization improves 10% on a relative basis. Their effects multiply: 1.091 × 1.10 ≈ 1.20.

This solution uses the 60-package monthly gap that already existed, then extracts more useful work from each available accelerator. It does not require more wafer or HBM output yet.

6. Check where the bottleneck moves

After the change, deployment and working-package output both equal 720. There is no longer spare package flow. The next expansion must improve package attempts, package yield or both, while staying below the 900 HBM-set limit.

For example, sustaining 792 deployments at unchanged 90% package yield would require:

792 working packages ÷ 90% yield = 880 package attempts

That still fits under the illustrative supplies of 900 HBM sets and 1,000 compute sets. A further increase could move the bottleneck to HBM.

Quick check — answer before reading on

Management announces 20% more wafer capacity and predicts 20% more useful AI, while every other baseline number stays fixed. Is the prediction supported?

Answer: no. Deployment remains 660 and runtime efficiency is unchanged, so useful output remains 43,890 work units. The tempting mistake is to apply one upstream growth rate to the entire chain.

Remember these three things

- Define useful AI output with a unit, time period and performance requirement.

- Total output is constrained by the tightest physical stage and then reduced by availability and ineffective runtime time.

- A successful capacity plan identifies the current bottleneck, changes it and predicts where the limit moves next.

